13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1a",null,{"guide":{"date":"August 28–September 25, 1862; fighting and siege at Munfordville, September 14–17","place":"The Confederate line of advance from Chattanooga through Sparta, Glasgow, and Cave City to Munfordville and Woodsonville at the Green River crossing of the Louisville & Nashville Railroad, Kentucky","result":"Confederate tactical victory but an unrealized strategic opportunity. James R. Chalmers’s unsupported September 14 assault failed with heavy losses; Braxton Bragg then surrounded the reinforced United States garrison and accepted John T. Wilder’s surrender on September 17. Short of food and forage, Bragg abandoned the commanding Green River position on September 20 and opened Buell’s road to Louisville","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"Bragg’s advance, the capture of Munfordville, and the decision to leave","note":"The episode follows the Army of the Mississippi from Chattanooga to the Green River, describes the September 14 assault and September 16–17 surrender, and closes with Bragg’s withdrawal toward Bardstown and Buell’s arrival at Louisville."},{"label":"Campaign","value":"Confederate Heartland Offensive","note":"Bragg moved north from Tennessee while Edmund Kirby Smith operated independently in eastern and central Kentucky."},{"label":"Strategic objective","value":"The Green River bridge and Buell’s supply line","note":"The Louisville & Nashville Railroad and turnpike crossed the Green River at Munfordville. Holding the crossing placed Bragg between the Army of the Ohio and its principal supply base at Louisville."},{"label":"Bragg’s army","value":"About 30,000 men when reorganized at Chattanooga","note":"The episode describes two wings under Leonidas Polk and William J. Hardee, each with two infantry divisions, plus roughly 6,000 cavalry divided between John C. Wharton and Joseph Wheeler."},{"label":"Command burden","value":"Bragg acted as both army commander and chief of staff","note":"His chief of staff left because of illness before the campaign. Bragg also divided his cavalry between wing commanders, leaving no mounted reserve directly under army headquarters."},{"label":"Buell’s problem","value":"A slow advance on a vulnerable railroad supply line","note":"Confederate cavalry raids, railroad repair, hunger, and Buell’s restrictions on foraging delayed the Army of the Ohio across northern Mississippi, Alabama, and Tennessee."},{"label":"Bragg leaves Chattanooga","value":"August 28, 1862","note":"He moved two days before Kirby Smith’s victory at Richmond, Kentucky, crossed the Cumberland Plateau, and reached Sparta on September 6."},{"label":"Glasgow concentration","value":"September 13, 1862","note":"Bragg’s army reached the Glasgow area, about twelve miles south of Cave City and roughly thirty-five miles east of Bowling Green."},{"label":"Fort Craig","value":"Redoubts, rifle pits, trenches, and a south-bank stockade","note":"United States troops had fortified the bridge approaches. The works south of the Green River protected both the railroad span and nearby Munfordville."},{"label":"Initial garrison","value":"2,122 men for duty in Wilder’s official report","note":"The command included the 67th and 89th Indiana, detachments of the 17th and 74th Indiana, one company of the 18th U.S. Infantry, Kentucky troops, cavalry, and four artillery pieces."},{"label":"Faulty reconnaissance","value":"John S. Scott reported 1,200–1,800 raw troops in weak works","note":"Scott also claimed that reinforcements could not arrive. The railroad and telegraph still connected the garrison with the north, and the fortifications were stronger than his description suggested."},{"label":"First surrender demand","value":"September 13, about 8 p.m.","note":"Scott demanded unconditional surrender from the north side of the river. Wilder refused."},{"label":"Chalmers acts on his own","value":"About 2,500 Mississippians marched from Cave City at 10 p.m.","note":"Chalmers did not wait for Bragg’s main army. He planned a converging attack before dawn on September 14."},{"label":"Assault plan","value":"10th and 44th Mississippi west; 7th, 9th, and 29th Mississippi east","note":"Richards’s sharpshooters opened against the pickets, four guns initially occupied Lewis’s Hill, and the 44th Mississippi began as the brigade reserve."},{"label":"Plan breaks down","value":"The attack began before every Confederate unit was in position","note":"United States pickets withdrew into the works, the first Confederate gun position lacked range, and burning buildings were mistaken for a signal to attack."},{"label":"Western attack","value":"The 10th Mississippi was pinned before the works","note":"The regiment advanced across exposed and obstructed ground; the 44th Mississippi went forward in support. Both lost senior officers under concentrated infantry and artillery fire."},{"label":"Eastern attack","value":"The 7th, 9th, and 29th Mississippi failed before Fort Craig","note":"United States fire checked the charge, and artillery sent by Scott mistakenly fired into Chalmers’s troops, increasing the confusion."},{"label":"September 14 losses","value":"285 Confederate casualties; 37 United States casualties","note":"These are the National Park Service battle-detail totals for the failed assault. Wilder’s contemporary report also placed his killed and wounded at 37."},{"label":"Dunham arrives","value":"Six companies of the 50th Indiana reached the works during the battle","note":"Their train stopped about six miles away. Roughly 400 men marched by a circuitous route, avoided Scott’s cavalry, and entered the defenses; Cyrus L. Dunham then took command as senior officer."},{"label":"A stronger garrison","value":"About 4,000 defenders by September 16","note":"Reinforcements arrived while Dunham and Wilder strengthened the works during September 15 and 16."},{"label":"Bragg concentrates","value":"More than 20,000 Confederates and dozens of guns","note":"Bragg’s September 16 message claimed a force exceeding 20,000. Wilder later reported counting forty-five cannon and being surrounded by more than 25,000 men."},{"label":"Encirclement","value":"Polk north of the Green River; Hardee south of it","note":"Polk’s right wing occupied commanding river hills on the north bank while Hardee’s left wing took the heights south of the fortifications."},{"label":"A command crisis","value":"Gilbert relieved Dunham and restored Wilder","note":"After Dunham telegraphed Louisville about the garrison’s doubtful prospects, acting Maj. Gen. Charles C. Gilbert ordered Wilder back into command and told Dunham to place himself under arrest."},{"label":"Council of war","value":"The officers would resist unless Bragg proved overwhelming strength","note":"With limited small-arms ammunition, exhausted men, and no relief force near enough to help, Wilder asked for evidence that further defense would be a useless loss of life."},{"label":"Buckner’s role","value":"A former surrendering commander guided Wilder through the Confederate lines","note":"Simon B. Buckner, a Munfordville native who had surrendered Fort Donelson in February, showed the blindfolded Wilder enough of Bragg’s position to establish the disparity in force."},{"label":"Surrender","value":"Agreed about 2 a.m.; formal march-out at 6 a.m., September 17","note":"Approximately 4,000 United States troops stacked arms. Bragg reported taking an equal number of small arms, ten artillery pieces, and large quantities of munitions; the prisoners were paroled."},{"label":"Battlefield total","value":"4,433 estimated casualties in the NPS table","note":"The National Park Service counts 4,148 United States losses—overwhelmingly surrendered—and 285 Confederate losses. Other summaries combine or estimate categories differently."},{"label":"The apparent opportunity","value":"Bragg stood astride Buell’s road to Louisville","note":"The Green River offered strong defensive ground, and Bragg initially expected Buell to attack. Buell’s army was then moving north from Bowling Green."},{"label":"The logistical limit","value":"The army could remain only a few days without starving","note":"Drought had exhausted men and animals, and the long-standing garrison had already stripped the nearby country of food and forage. Bragg could neither sustain the position nor spare enough strength to attack Buell safely."},{"label":"Bragg abandons Munfordville","value":"September 20, 1862","note":"The army moved northeast toward Bardstown in search of supplies and a junction with Kirby Smith. The Confederates destroyed the railroad bridge as they left."},{"label":"Buell reaches Louisville","value":"September 25, 1862","note":"Once Bragg left the Green River line, the Army of the Ohio had a clear route to Louisville, where it could refit and absorb reinforcements."},{"label":"Transcript corrections","value":"Fort Donelson; Decherd; brevetted; Chalmers’s brigade; Col. Robert A. Smith; Lt. Col. James Moore","note":"The automated transcript says “Fort Donaldson,” “Deckard,” “brevity,” and “British Brigade.” It also says “Colonel Scott” was killed in the 10th Mississippi attack: the officer was Col. Robert A. Smith, who was mortally wounded. The 44th Mississippi’s acting commander, Lt. Col. James Moore, was mortally wounded; the regiment retained the name of Col. A. K. Blythe, who had been killed at Shiloh."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"All for the Regiment: The Army of the Ohio, 1861–1862","note":"Gerald J. Prokopowicz examines the Army of the Ohio from its organization through the Kentucky campaign and Perryville. The automated transcript misspells the author’s surname as “Prokopovich.”"}],"maps":[{"title":"Munfordville: assault, encirclement, and withdrawal","caption":"North-up schematic of the Green River crossing. Chalmers’s September 14 attack approached Fort Craig from the south and southwest while Scott’s cavalry operated north of the river; Scott’s supporting guns mistakenly fired into Confederate infantry. On September 16, Hardee occupied the south-bank hills and Polk the commanding north-bank heights. Bragg accepted the garrison’s surrender on September 17, then moved toward Bardstown on September 20.","source":"Custom diagram based on the Official Records, the National Park Service battle and historic-district documentation, and American Battlefield Trust phase maps; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep209-munfordville-campaign-map.svg","alt":"North-up schematic map showing Munfordville north of the Green River, Fort Craig and Woodsonville south of the river, Confederate attacks from the south, the September 16 encirclement, and Bragg’s route toward Bardstown."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:01","audioTime":60.59,"title":"The campaign resumes after Richmond","detail":"Kirby Smith’s victory opened Lexington, which his Army of Kentucky entered on September 2 before sending detachments toward Louisville and Cincinnati."},{"time":"1:28","audioTime":87.63,"title":"Smith occupies the Bluegrass","detail":"The episode describes the welcome at Lexington and the September 4 capture of Frankfort, the only loyal-state capital occupied by Confederate troops during the war."},{"time":"2:13","audioTime":132.99,"title":"Bragg prepares to follow","detail":"Troop movements from Mississippi delayed Bragg at Chattanooga until late August, leaving Smith to begin the Kentucky advance first."},{"time":"2:53","audioTime":173.31,"title":"Braxton Bragg introduced","detail":"The episode traces the army commander’s West Point education, Regular Army career, Mexican War service, plantation years, and rapid Confederate promotion."},{"time":"5:59","audioTime":358.33,"title":"The Chattanooga plan comes apart","detail":"Bragg and Kirby Smith had agreed to cooperate in Middle Tennessee, but Smith redirected his column toward Kentucky and Bragg chose to follow the new strategy."},{"time":"7:18","audioTime":438.49,"title":"Bragg reorganizes the army","detail":"The Army of the Mississippi formed a right wing under Leonidas Polk and a left wing under William J. Hardee, each containing two divisions."},{"time":"9:08","audioTime":548.25,"title":"Cavalry and staff arrangements weaken headquarters","detail":"Bragg divided the cavalry between wing commanders and assumed the duties of his ailing chief of staff in addition to army command."},{"time":"10:01","audioTime":601.03,"title":"Buell’s difficult summer","detail":"The Army of the Ohio struggled toward Chattanooga while Confederate cavalry raids, damaged railroads, and shortages slowed its advance."},{"time":"12:05","audioTime":725.05,"title":"Railroad repair governs the advance","detail":"Henry Halleck had ordered Buell to rebuild the Memphis & Charleston Railroad as his army moved east from Corinth."},{"time":"14:14","audioTime":853.67,"title":"Buell begins to concentrate","detail":"Reports of Kirby Smith’s movement and Bragg’s intentions led Buell to draw his scattered army toward Decherd, Tennessee, on August 19."},{"time":"15:23","audioTime":922.61,"title":"Bragg leaves Chattanooga","detail":"The Army of the Mississippi began its march on August 28 and crossed the Cumberland Plateau toward Sparta."},{"time":"16:16","audioTime":976.49,"title":"The Confederate column enters Kentucky","detail":"Bragg moved through Tompkinsville and reached the Glasgow area by September 13."},{"time":"16:35","audioTime":995.01,"title":"Buell’s supply line is exposed","detail":"The Louisville & Nashville Railroad tied Buell’s forward army to its principal depot at Louisville."},{"time":"17:11","audioTime":1031.33,"title":"Munfordville controls the Green River crossing","detail":"The turnpike and railroad crossed at this fortified point twelve miles north of Cave City."},{"time":"17:38","audioTime":1057.65,"title":"Wilder commands Fort Craig","detail":"About 2,000 United States infantry and several guns defended the bridge approaches behind redoubts and connecting trenches."},{"time":"18:07","audioTime":1086.77,"title":"Scott demands surrender","detail":"On the evening of September 13, Confederate cavalry commander John S. Scott called on the garrison to capitulate; Wilder refused."},{"time":"18:45","audioTime":1124.85,"title":"Chalmers chooses an immediate attack","detail":"Acting on Scott’s inaccurate description of the defenses and garrison, Chalmers decided to strike without waiting for Bragg’s main army."},{"time":"19:05","audioTime":1145.33,"title":"The Mississippi brigade marches overnight","detail":"About 2,500 men left Cave City at 10 p.m. and deployed before Fort Craig ahead of dawn on September 14."},{"time":"19:53","audioTime":1192.97,"title":"The first shots","detail":"Richards’s sharpshooters engaged Wilder’s pickets as the Confederate assault began around 5 a.m."},{"time":"20:17","audioTime":1217.39,"title":"Confederate guns lack range","detail":"Artillery on Lewis’s Hill had to move closer, where United States cannon could answer effectively."},{"time":"20:35","audioTime":1235.27,"title":"A mistaken signal starts the western attack","detail":"Col. Robert A. Smith interpreted burning buildings near the defenses as the signal and sent the 10th Mississippi forward."},{"time":"20:46","audioTime":1245.83,"title":"The 10th Mississippi is pinned down","detail":"Concentrated fire stopped the regiment before the works. Smith was mortally wounded, and Lt. Col. James G. Bullard was killed."},{"time":"20:56","audioTime":1256.37,"title":"The 44th Mississippi joins the costly attack","detail":"Blythe’s regiment advanced in support. Its acting commander, Lt. Col. James Moore, was mortally wounded."},{"time":"21:27","audioTime":1286.67,"title":"Chalmers sends in the eastern regiments","detail":"The 9th and 29th Mississippi advanced, and the 7th moved forward to support them against Fort Craig."},{"time":"21:53","audioTime":1312.57,"title":"Friendly artillery fire breaks the assault","detail":"Fire from Scott’s supporting guns landed among Chalmers’s own infantry and helped destroy the coordination of the attack."},{"time":"22:08","audioTime":1327.81,"title":"The failed attack ends","detail":"By about 9:30 a.m., after more than four hours of fighting, Chalmers withdrew from the immediate defenses."},{"time":"22:29","audioTime":1349.11,"title":"The casualty disparity is clear","detail":"The episode gives Chalmers 285 killed, wounded, and missing against 37 casualties in Wilder’s command."},{"time":"22:33","audioTime":1352.51,"title":"Wilder refuses again","detail":"When Chalmers renewed his surrender demand, Wilder answered that he would defend the position until overpowered."},{"time":"23:06","audioTime":1385.59,"title":"Dunham reaches the fort","detail":"Cyrus L. Dunham arrived with about 400 men of the 50th Indiana and assumed command as the senior officer."},{"time":"23:41","audioTime":1420.83,"title":"The garrison doubles","detail":"Reinforcements raised the defending force to about 4,000 by the morning of September 16."},{"time":"24:17","audioTime":1457.17,"title":"Buckner supplies local knowledge","detail":"The Munfordville native helped Bragg plan the investment of the fortified river crossing."},{"time":"24:25","audioTime":1465.01,"title":"Bragg brings up the army","detail":"The Army of the Mississippi moved from Glasgow late on September 15 and reached the Munfordville defenses the next day."},{"time":"24:38","audioTime":1478.27,"title":"Polk and Hardee close the ring","detail":"Hardee’s troops occupied the south-bank approaches while Polk crossed upstream and placed guns on the north-bank heights."},{"time":"24:48","audioTime":1488.39,"title":"The disparity becomes overwhelming","detail":"By late afternoon, roughly 25,000 Confederates and a ring of artillery surrounded the 4,000-man garrison."},{"time":"25:00","audioTime":1499.61,"title":"Bragg demands surrender","detail":"Dunham initially refused, saying that he would defend the works to the utmost despite the consequences of an assault."},{"time":"25:34","audioTime":1534.07,"title":"A truce pauses the crisis","detail":"The opposing commanders agreed to stop firing until 9 p.m. while the garrison reconsidered its position."},{"time":"25:39","audioTime":1539.21,"title":"Dunham telegraphs Louisville","detail":"The line remained open long enough for Dunham to report his doubts to acting Maj. Gen. Charles C. Gilbert."},{"time":"25:58","audioTime":1557.55,"title":"Wilder returns to command","detail":"Gilbert relieved Dunham, restored Wilder, and ordered Dunham under arrest; Dunham offered to fight in the trenches as a private soldier."},{"time":"26:57","audioTime":1616.95,"title":"The officers set a condition for resistance","detail":"They agreed to continue only unless Bragg could demonstrate that his superiority made defense a useless sacrifice."},{"time":"27:49","audioTime":1668.81,"title":"Buckner takes charge of the negotiations","detail":"Having surrendered Fort Donelson himself, Buckner understood Wilder’s dilemma and offered to show him the Confederate position."},{"time":"28:55","audioTime":1735.29,"title":"Wilder crosses the lines","detail":"Around 2 a.m., the blindfolded United States commander entered Buckner’s tent and asked what he ought to do."},{"time":"29:56","audioTime":1795.93,"title":"Wilder decides to surrender","detail":"After seeing the Confederate positions and considering whether resistance would help Buell elsewhere, Wilder chose capitulation."},{"time":"30:28","audioTime":1828.09,"title":"The garrison capitulates","detail":"In the early hours of September 17, Wilder surrendered approximately 4,000 troops without a renewed assault."},{"time":"30:49","audioTime":1849.26,"title":"One of the war’s larger surrenders","detail":"At 6 a.m., the garrison marched out and stacked arms; the prisoners were paroled and sent toward United States lines."},{"time":"31:18","audioTime":1878.76,"title":"Bragg wins his first victory in army command","detail":"He reported about 4,000 prisoners, an equal number of small arms, ten cannon, and large quantities of military stores."},{"time":"32:17","audioTime":1937.08,"title":"A powerful tactical position","detail":"Bragg now stood between Buell and Louisville with a defensible river barrier across the Army of the Ohio’s route."},{"time":"32:47","audioTime":1967.48,"title":"Buell closes from Bowling Green","detail":"The Army of the Ohio was already north of Nashville and concentrating around Bowling Green as Munfordville fell."},{"time":"33:24","audioTime":2004.44,"title":"The armies probe toward each other","detail":"Buell approached cautiously because the Green River was a serious obstacle and his estimates exaggerated Bragg’s strength."},{"time":"34:03","audioTime":2042.96,"title":"Logistics undo the position","detail":"The drought-stricken Confederate army found little food or forage around a post whose garrison had already consumed local supplies."},{"time":"34:49","audioTime":2089.18,"title":"The missed opportunity debated","detail":"Later criticism focused on Bragg’s decision to give up the Green River line, but the episode argues that his supply condition left few practical choices."},{"time":"35:02","audioTime":2102.28,"title":"Bragg marches for Bardstown","detail":"On September 20 the Confederates abandoned Munfordville, seeking food and a junction with Kirby Smith farther northeast."},{"time":"35:22","audioTime":2122.48,"title":"Buell reaches Louisville","detail":"With the route open, the Army of the Ohio arrived at Kentucky’s largest city on September 25."},{"time":"36:02","audioTime":2162.24,"title":"The episode’s reading recommendation","detail":"Gerald J. Prokopowicz’s All for the Regiment follows the Army of the Ohio through the campaigns that culminated at Perryville."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315a88","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Don Carlos Buell","command":"Army of the Ohio","subordinates":"About 55,000 men after reinforcement; approaching from Nashville through Bowling Green but not present in the Munfordville works"},{"commander":"Acting Maj. Gen. Charles C. Gilbert","command":"Headquarters at Louisville","subordinates":"Controlled the garrison by telegraph; relieved Dunham and restored Wilder on the evening of September 16"},{"commander":"Col. John T. Wilder","command":"Munfordville garrison, September 8–14 and again September 16–17","subordinates":"67th and 89th Indiana; detachments of the 17th and 74th Indiana; one company, 18th U.S. Infantry; 33rd Kentucky detachment; cavalry and artillery"},{"commander":"Col. Cyrus L. Dunham","command":"Munfordville garrison, September 14–16","subordinates":"Six companies of the 50th Indiana and additional reinforcements; senior officer after reaching Fort Craig during the September 14 battle"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#982f2f","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Braxton Bragg","command":"Army of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Roughly 30,000 men when the campaign began; more than 20,000 concentrated around Munfordville by September 16"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Leonidas Polk","command":"Right wing","subordinates":"Jones M. Withers’s and Benjamin F. Cheatham’s divisions; occupied the north-bank heights during the encirclement"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. James R. Chalmers","command":"Second Brigade, Withers’s Division","subordinates":"7th, 9th, 10th, 29th, and 44th Mississippi; Richards’s sharpshooters; Gage’s battery; conducted the September 14 assault"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. William J. Hardee","command":"Left wing","subordinates":"J. Patton Anderson’s and Simon B. Buckner’s divisions; occupied the south-bank approaches on September 16"},{"commander":"Col. John S. Scott","command":"Cavalry brigade from Kirby Smith’s Army of Kentucky","subordinates":"Screened the north side of Green River, supplied faulty intelligence to Chalmers, and sent artillery that mistakenly fired into the September 14 attack"}]}],"people":[{"name":"John T. Wilder","role":"United States colonel who built the Munfordville defenses, repulsed Chalmers, returned to command on September 16, and surrendered after inspecting Bragg’s overwhelming position","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep209-john-t-wilder.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2023631560/"},{"name":"Cyrus L. Dunham","role":"United States colonel who brought part of the 50th Indiana into Fort Craig and commanded the enlarged garrison from September 14 until Gilbert relieved him","side":"United States","source":"https://texashistory.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metapth154624/m1/967/"},{"name":"Don Carlos Buell","role":"Commander of the Army of the Ohio, marching north from Tennessee through Bowling Green toward Louisville","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=ky008"},{"name":"Charles C. Gilbert","role":"Acting major general at Louisville who restored Wilder to command after Dunham’s pessimistic telegraph","side":"United States","source":"https://texashistory.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metapth154624/m1/978/"},{"name":"Augustus H. Abbott","role":"Major who rallied defenders on the Fort Craig parapet and was killed beneath the flag during the September 14 assault","side":"United States","source":"https://texashistory.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metapth154624/m1/968/"},{"name":"Braxton Bragg","role":"Confederate army commander who concentrated at Munfordville, forced the surrender, and then abandoned the position because of logistics","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep209-braxton-bragg.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2022630536/"},{"name":"Leonidas Polk","role":"Confederate right-wing commander whose troops crossed upstream and occupied the heights north of Green River","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://texashistory.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metapth154624/m1/970/"},{"name":"William J. Hardee","role":"Confederate left-wing commander whose troops occupied the south-bank approaches during the encirclement","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://texashistory.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metapth154624/m1/970/"},{"name":"James R. Chalmers","role":"Confederate brigadier general who launched the unsuccessful September 14 assault without waiting for Bragg’s main army","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://texashistory.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metapth154624/m1/979/"},{"personId":"simon-b-buckner","role":"Munfordville-born Confederate division commander who escorted Wilder through Bragg’s lines and helped arrange the surrender"},{"name":"John S. Scott","role":"Confederate cavalry commander whose faulty report encouraged Chalmers’s assault and whose supporting artillery fired into Confederate infantry","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://texashistory.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metapth154624/m1/982/"},{"name":"Robert A. Smith","role":"Colonel of the 10th Mississippi, mortally wounded while leading the western attack against Fort Craig","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://finding.mdah.ms.gov/manuscripts/z0622"}],"losses":[{"name":"Maj. Augustus H. Abbott","side":"United States","role":"Field officer in the Munfordville garrison","outcome":"Killed on the Fort Craig parapet while rallying the defenders during the September 14 assault."},{"name":"Col. Robert A. Smith","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commander, 10th Mississippi Infantry","outcome":"Mortally wounded while leading the regiment across obstructed ground against the western works; died during the Munfordville operation."},{"name":"Lt. Col. James G. Bullard","side":"Confederacy","role":"Lieutenant colonel, 10th Mississippi Infantry","outcome":"Killed in the 10th Mississippi’s September 14 attack against Fort Craig."},{"name":"Lt. Col. James Moore","side":"Confederacy","role":"Acting commander, Blythe’s 44th Mississippi Infantry","outcome":"Mortally wounded while leading the 44th Mississippi in support of the 10th Mississippi’s attack."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep209-munfordville-campaign-map.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the Green River crossing, Confederate assault routes, siege positions, and withdrawal from Munfordville.","caption":"Fort Craig guarded the bridge south of the Green River. Chalmers’s piecemeal attack failed, but Bragg’s whole army later occupied commanding heights on both banks and forced the garrison to surrender.","source":"Custom diagram based on Official Records, National Park Service documentation, and American Battlefield Trust phase maps."},{"image":"/images/ep209-john-t-wilder.jpg","alt":"Full-length studio portrait of United States officer John T. Wilder in uniform.","caption":"John T. Wilder prepared the defenses, rejected repeated demands on September 13 and 14, and capitulated only after Bragg demonstrated overwhelming strength.","source":"Liljenquist Family Collection of Civil War Photographs, Library of Congress; no known restrictions on publication."},{"image":"/images/ep209-braxton-bragg.jpg","alt":"Full-length studio portrait of Confederate Maj. Gen. Braxton Bragg in uniform.","caption":"Braxton Bragg turned Chalmers’s costly reverse into a large surrender, then relinquished the Green River line when his army could not live off the exhausted countryside.","source":"Liljenquist Family Collection of Civil War Photographs, Library of Congress; nineteenth-century photograph."},{"image":"/images/ep209-simon-b-buckner.jpg","alt":"Full-length studio portrait of Confederate Lt. Gen. Simon Bolivar Buckner in uniform.","caption":"Simon B. Buckner knew the terrain around his hometown and, drawing on his own experience at Fort Donelson, treated Wilder’s surrender decision with unusual candor.","source":"Liljenquist Family Collection of Civil War Photographs, Library of Congress; no known restrictions on publication."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Munfordville battle detail","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=ky008"},{"label":"Primary source: Official Records, Wilder’s report of the siege and surrender","url":"https://texashistory.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metapth154624/m1/967/"},{"label":"Primary source: Official Records, surrender correspondence and Bragg’s report","url":"https://texashistory.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metapth154624/m1/976/"},{"label":"Primary source: Official Records, Chalmers’s September 14 operations report","url":"https://texashistory.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metapth154624/m1/982/"},{"label":"National Park Service/National Register: Battle of Munfordville district","url":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/NARAprodstorage/lz/electronic-records/rg-079/NPS_KY/97000866.pdf"},{"label":"American Battlefield Trust: Munfordville phase maps","url":"https://www.battlefields.org/learn/maps/munfordville-sept-14-17-1862"},{"label":"Kentucky Historical Society: Battle of Munfordville marker","url":"https://history.ky.gov/markers/battle-of-munfordville"},{"label":"Mississippi Department of Archives and History: Robert A. Smith papers","url":"https://finding.mdah.ms.gov/manuscripts/z0622"},{"label":"Library of Congress: John T. Wilder portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2023631560/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Braxton Bragg portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2022630536/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Simon B. Buckner portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2016649609/"}]}}]}]
14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"People and command"}],[[["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"className":"command-grid reference-command","children":[["$","section","United States",{"className":"command-column #315a88","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"United States"}],[["$","div","Army of the Ohio-Maj. Gen. Don Carlos Buell",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Army of the Ohio"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Don Carlos Buell"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"About 55,000 men after reinforcement; approaching from Nashville through Bowling Green but not present in the Munfordville works"}]]}],["$","div","Headquarters at Louisville-Acting Maj. Gen. Charles C. Gilbert",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Headquarters at Louisville"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Acting Maj. Gen. Charles C. Gilbert"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Controlled the garrison by telegraph; relieved Dunham and restored Wilder on the evening of September 16"}]]}],["$","div","Munfordville garrison, September 8–14 and again September 16–17-Col. John T. Wilder",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Munfordville garrison, September 8–14 and again September 16–17"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Col. John T. Wilder"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"67th and 89th Indiana; detachments of the 17th and 74th Indiana; one company, 18th U.S. Infantry; 33rd Kentucky detachment; cavalry and artillery"}]]}],["$","div","Munfordville garrison, September 14–16-Col. Cyrus L. Dunham",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Munfordville garrison, September 14–16"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Col. Cyrus L. Dunham"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Six companies of the 50th Indiana and additional reinforcements; senior officer after reaching Fort Craig during the September 14 battle"}]]}]]]}],["$","section","Confederacy",{"className":"command-column #982f2f","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Confederacy"}],[["$","div","Army of the Mississippi-Gen. Braxton Bragg",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Army of the Mississippi"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Gen. Braxton Bragg"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Roughly 30,000 men when the campaign began; more than 20,000 concentrated around Munfordville by September 16"}]]}],["$","div","Right wing-Maj. Gen. Leonidas Polk",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Right wing"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Leonidas Polk"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Jones M. Withers’s and Benjamin F. Cheatham’s divisions; occupied the north-bank heights during the encirclement"}]]}],["$","div","Second Brigade, Withers’s Division-Brig. Gen. James R. Chalmers",{"className":"command-entry","children":["$L1b","$L1c","$L1d"]}],"$L1e","$L1f"]]}]]}],"$L20"]]]}]
15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"losses","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Important leaders killed or wounded"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"loss-list","children":[["$","article","Maj. Augustus H. Abbott",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Maj. Augustus H. Abbott"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Field officer in the Munfordville garrison","."]}]," ","Killed on the Fort Craig parapet while rallying the defenders during the September 14 assault."]}]]}],["$","article","Col. Robert A. Smith",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side confederate","children":"Confederacy"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Col. Robert A. Smith"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Commander, 10th Mississippi Infantry","."]}]," ","Mortally wounded while leading the regiment across obstructed ground against the western works; died during the Munfordville operation."]}]]}],["$","article","Lt. Col. James G. Bullard",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side confederate","children":"Confederacy"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Lt. Col. James G. Bullard"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Lieutenant colonel, 10th Mississippi Infantry","."]}]," ","Killed in the 10th Mississippi’s September 14 attack against Fort Craig."]}]]}],["$","article","Lt. Col. James Moore",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side confederate","children":"Confederacy"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Lt. Col. James Moore"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Acting commander, Blythe’s 44th Mississippi Infantry","."]}]," ","Mortally wounded while leading the 44th Mississippi in support of the 10th Mississippi’s attack."]}]]}]]}]]}]
16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"images","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Historical images"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"history-gallery","children":[["$","$L21","/images/ep209-john-t-wilder.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep209-john-t-wilder.jpg","alt":"Full-length studio portrait of United States officer John T. Wilder in uniform.","caption":"John T. Wilder prepared the defenses, rejected repeated demands on September 13 and 14, and capitulated only after Bragg demonstrated overwhelming strength.","name":"Full-length studio portrait of United States officer John T. Wilder in uniform.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Liljenquist Family Collection of Civil War Photographs, Library of Congress; no known restrictions on publication."}],["$","$L21","/images/ep209-braxton-bragg.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep209-braxton-bragg.jpg","alt":"Full-length studio portrait of Confederate Maj. Gen. Braxton Bragg in uniform.","caption":"Braxton Bragg turned Chalmers’s costly reverse into a large surrender, then relinquished the Green River line when his army could not live off the exhausted countryside.","name":"Full-length studio portrait of Confederate Maj. Gen. Braxton Bragg in uniform.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Liljenquist Family Collection of Civil War Photographs, Library of Congress; nineteenth-century photograph."}],["$","$L21","/images/ep209-simon-b-buckner.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep209-simon-b-buckner.jpg","alt":"Full-length studio portrait of Confederate Lt. Gen. Simon Bolivar Buckner in uniform.","caption":"Simon B. Buckner knew the terrain around his hometown and, drawing on his own experience at Fort Donelson, treated Wilder’s surrender decision with unusual candor.","name":"Full-length studio portrait of Confederate Lt. Gen. Simon Bolivar Buckner in uniform.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Liljenquist Family Collection of Civil War Photographs, Library of Congress; no known restrictions on publication."}]]}]]}]
